A||||11||PaUL||Camber Adjusting Front Ball Joints
Z||000000||PaUL||04-09-99||02:26 PM||||Does anyone here have the adjustable front top ball joints from the Fiero Store/Leader Inds? These have slotted bolt holes and allow you to change the camber on the front wheels. I was thinking these would be good to fix the understeer problem on 84-87s. 
Z||000001||theogre||04-09-99||04:23 PM||||in one of the other posts, there are GM part numbers for longer upper arms. A couple places make a fully adjustable arm. I don't know how much adjustment the F.S. joints have.<p>My understanding it that you need between 1-2 degrees of change to correct the bump steer problem. That seems like allot for a joint to be able to provide. adjustable joints are usually for minor corrections due to a slightly bent arm or something like that.<p>If you are also lowering, then you about have to use a diferant arm. you also need sloted struts to correct the rear.
Z||000002||PaUL||04-09-99||05:21 PM||||Ogre, I dont want to lower my car, just want to make it understeer less. I figure I would need shorter upper arms not longer. I was thinking of changing the camber so the top of the wheel is tilted towards the inside (is that neg or pos camber?), I dont mind the uneven tire wear. 
Z||000003||theogre||04-10-99||08:50 PM||||the longer arm was deisgned, acording to my info, to correct that specific problem, not just for lowered cars. It's long enogh that it's about a requirement for a lowered car tho.<p>Bump steer is caused when one of the controll arms, or the tie rod, doesn't move thru a correct relative arc with the other parts. I call it a relative arc bucause the parts never move thru the same arc, they can't, or even thru the same radius. In a correctly designed system the combined arcs of the parts add up to move the wheel thru a consistant arc without rotating the wheel along the vertical axis.<p>In Fiero's case, the Upper arm aparently is too short pulling the wheel in when it is moved up or down, and this causes the wheel to rotate along it's vertical axis . This is bump steer. (it does it to both wheels evenly when conering, so you don't notice as much. In cornering it does increase toe in when this happens. which causes other problems.)
Z||000004||PaUL||04-16-99||05:28 PM||||Ogre,<br>Im putting in my new balljoints this weekend, tell me again why the fiero needs an effective longer top A arm. <p>I always used to watch indycar racing and their wheels have alot of camber visible even on TV. The tops of their wheels tilts towards the inside, I always thought this was to give the inside tires more bite when taking a turn. Now you were telling me that the top arm should be longer but wouldnt this have an opposite effect? <p>I dont know about the bump steer problem you described earlier, but on a smooth surface, seems like this: /---\ would be better than this: \---/. Please elaborate further.
Z||000005||PaUL||04-16-99||05:45 PM||||I mean the outside tire. Wouldnt the outside tire have more grip if its cambered so that the top tilts in?
Z||000006||theogre||04-17-99||02:50 PM||||paul, never compair a street car to a race car for adjustments. Indy, F1, etc, do exactly that, they pull in the tops. This is done both to imrove handling, and to improve tire life. Off road vehicles get even more extreme. Off roaders want the wheels to plow up the surface under the wheel when they turn.<p>Properly installed, the slightly longer arm does NOT push the wheels out. It allows the upper ball to swing thru a larger arc, but the inboard end is some what farther in toward the car's center than the stock arm. (most upper arms are in slots on the inboard end, I do believe that applies to Fiero to.) The actual alignment of the wheels at rest Does Not change. The end effect is to make the upper ball swing thru an arc much closer to what the lower ball, and the tie rod end are using.<p>Bump steer is caused when one of the three joint arcs on the wheel spindle unit is signifigantly greater or smaller than the other 2. instead of the wheel moving cleanly along its vertical axis, it is jerked in/out by the offending joint causing the wheel to twist as it moves. The end result is that the wheel pulls in some direction just as if you turned the steering wheel. Unlike when you turn the wheel, bump steer is an explosively instant effect on the front end of the car. Severe bump steer Can rip the steering wheel right out of your hand.<p>It's important to remember that bump steer can never be eliminated completely. All cars have some. It's a tradeoff between that, and other handling factors. It part of why some of the most responcive cars can also be some of the most unstable one. Same way even with aircraft. Any improvemnt in handling results in something else being traded. Ride stiffness for example. A car with a soft riding suspesion rarly handles as well as the exact same car with a stiffer setup.<p>Without having one of your new moog joints, I don't know if you get enough adjustment to help with bump steer or not.<p>even a 1-1.5" lowering would prety much require the longer arm. no way a slotted joint will be enough.
Z||000007||PaUL||04-18-99||08:01 PM||||Ogre, I just put the balljoints in. I must say the amount of camber adjustment these allow is big enough to be noticable with the wheels on and car lowered. I adjusted them exactly the opposite of what you said, cambered with the tops tilted in as much as the slots would allow. I drove it for a short distance and didnt encounter any increased bumpsteer. It feels real solid, with less noise going over bumps, before it would make clunking sounds. Ill have to test this setup out for awhile but I might adjust them back out.
Z||000008||theogre||04-18-99||09:29 PM||||I strongly recomend you get to a shop, and get that thing aligned. tell them you just replaced the joints, and let them get those wheel where they belong.
Z||000009||PaUL||04-23-99||05:43 PM||||Ogre,<p>After reading your replies I did some research on suspension geometry. I went to a website that featured an article on improving the suspension. Here is quote:<p>"The camber adjustment on the front of a Fiero is accomplished by rotating the upper ball joints from front to the back.  The ball joint is offset between the mounting bolts so you have your choice of two positions.  For the best handling, you want the position that gives the most negative camber.  Usually this isn't much, so you'll need to move the holes to get more.  I don't recommend more then one degree of negative camber for street use."<p>Now doesnt that totally contradict what you just wrote above? All I can say is that my car handles much better with more neg camber. The front tires dont squeal at all no matter how fast I try taking turns, but Ive been afraid to push the limit. Steering feel and straight line stability also seems improved. Like I said before, Im will to trade tire wear for better front end grip. <p>Here's another quote I found on the subject from the maker of A Arm Suspension Geo. Pro (PC susp. software):<p><br>"On most newer production cars, when the body leans to the out side in a turn, the wheels typically lean half as much as the body, but it is still in the wrong direction to take advantage of the camber thrust. Most racers use more static camber to compensate for the loss of camber in the turns"<br>
Z||000010||theogre||04-25-99||09:29 PM||||how about posting both those links.... I'm probably not the only one who'd like to read those.<p>as for having an adjustment backwards.... I just may in some cases... especially with some of the zooyness in my life the last couple weeks. at any rate, there's time you want them pulled in, others out. There are also times you want to fool with the caster, and toe adjustments. <p>at any rate, I think you should have started with a complete alignment after adding the new joints. the car likely needed it anyway and without it you may be masking other places that are out of adjustment. at the very least, you have no reference point.<p>I like to make adjustments to all sorts of things, my computer motherboard for example is running at 75mhz, it should be 66. But I always try to make sure I have a reference point. that way if the adjustment doesn't work I know where I started. (for sake of the same example,,, I tryed over clocking the CPU only, but it didn't make as much of an improvent as kicking the motherboard up, while leaving the cpu pretty close to it's norm.)<p>as for performance. My 87SC is bone stock. I've skwalked in a turn exactly twice since I had it. once on wet road, and once while slight breaking into a full lock turn. and I've only got the 195/75 14"s on it.
Z||000011||PaUL||04-26-99||01:58 PM||||I like to make adjustments on all kinds of things too but some are willing to push their hardware to the limit and some are not. My motherboard bus speed is 100mhz with a 4.5 multiplier, that makes my cpu speed 450mhz. Thats from a cpu rated to run only at 300mhz. I got this knowledge for building a dirt cheap 450mhz speed demon on the the net. The board and cpu cost me less than $200. As with my computer, I like to "adjust" my car in the same way, get info on the net and figure out the best, most cost effective way to improve it.  <p>here's the link, its a website from a member of this forum:<br> <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/shaun41178/" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178/</A> <br>
